                          HOW TO USE OBJECTS IN PASCALINE

Pascaline implements objects as an extention to the module concept, similar to
the language Java, but unlike C++, Oberon and similar languages that consider 
objects to be extended records. In practice, this makes little difference
except minor syntax variations and that module based classes can do more and
are more flexible than record based objects.

CLASSES ARE MODULES (AND MORE)

A module in Pascaline is a container for constants, types, variables, procedures
and other definitions. A procedure or a function is also such a container. The
difference between procedures or functions and modules is that a procedure
allows external access through the "top" of the block:

           +----- Parameter list <------> External access
           |
   ----------------
   |              |
   | Declarations |
   |    	      |
   | Block        |
   |              |
   ----------------

A procedure or function allows access via the parameter list. A module:

   ----------------
   |              |
   | Declarations <--------> External access
   |    	      |
   | Block        |
   |              |
   ----------------

Allows access through the "side" of the block via the declarations. If a block
is bonded via "uses", it is in fact bonded to the module that uses it 
symbolically.

A class is first and foremost a module, and is bonded to external blocks by the
side. However, classes only have "joins" level bonding, meaning that all 
accesses to class declarations must be qualidents such as:

var a: myclass.newtype;

In a module, var declarations are "static". That is, they are allocated at a
fixed location and are not created anew with invocation, as procedure and 
function locals are.

A class does not create such declarations as static. In fact, a class defines
no variable data at all. As it is, a class only defines a "template" for the
appearance of a block of data. Lets look at a typical, but useful, class:

class list;

var next: reference to list;

begin { constructor }
end.

The class "list" is only a description of how to layout a block of data that
constitutes a list. In order to actually create the block of data that is a 
list, we have to create a reference to the list element. This is an object of
the type list:

var rp: reference to list;

And as you can see, the list class contains such a reference within itself.

One further thing to note for this example is that the list class contains a
constructor, abeit an empty one. The syntax of a class actually allows the
constructor to be omitted, but I make it a habit of including a constructor
block even if it is empty, because it clearly shows the constructor and marks
the end of the class.

CLASSES AS A CONTAINER FOR DECLARATIONS

When simply serving in their role as an extended module, the class identifier
itself can be used to access most of its definitions via a "qualident":

program test;

class myclass;

const x = 10;

type y = array x of char;

fixed q: integer = 10;

procedure z; begin end;

begin { constructor }
end.

var x: array myclass.x of integer;
    y: myclass.y;

begin

   x[5] := myclass.q;
   myclass.z

end.

In fact, the only class members that cannot be referenced via the class 
identifier are variables and certain procedures and functions (described later).

CLASS REFERENCES

The variable data in a class does not exist in the class declaration itself. A
class is simply a template. To create such data, the class must be "instatiated"
as an object:

program test;

class myclass;

var i: integer;

begin { constructor }
end.

var r: reference to myclass;

begin

   r := nil;
   new(r);
   r.i := 10;
   dispose(r);

end.

First, a reference variable must be created that points to the object that will
be created.	This is then assigned to nil (or "no object"). To create (or 
"instantiate" an object, and point the reference variable at that, the "new(r)"
code is used. This allocates the space for the object from dynamic storage, and
causes r to point to it.

A reference can point to the data in an object, and the access to the object is
automatically coded when a qualident for the referenced object appears. 
References also have a property known as "grace" that allows them to access the
other parts of a class as well. Therefore:

program test;

class myclass;

const x = 10;

type ref = reference to myclass;

var i: integer;

begin { constructor }
end.

var	r: myclass.ref;

begin

   new(r);
   r.i := r.x;

end.

In this case, the reference variable r has been used both to access the variable
i and to access the constant x. Grace is both a convienence and a functional
property of references. For example, the definition of myclass could later be
rewritten as:

program test;

class myclass;

type ref = reference to myclass;

var i: integer;
    x: integer;

begin { constructor }

   x := 10

end.

var	r: myclass.ref;

begin

   new(r);
   r.i := r.x;

end.

This is functionally equivalent to the first example, except that x has been
promoted from a constant to a variable that has been initialized in advance.

Note also that we have included in myclass its own reference type. This is a
common idiom for classes in Pascaline.

Classes are often compared to records in that they join multiple data types into
a whole. In Pascaline, the exact order of the elements are undefined, and the
types of members that a class can contain is significantly expanded to include
constants, types, fixed as well as variables and methods (procedures and 
functions).

REFERENCES AS DATA

A reference is a variable, and can be used anywhere a normal variable can.
Specifically, it can appear in a structure, passed as a parameter, returned as
the result of a function, read and written to a file, allocated as a dynamic
variable, etc:

program test;

class myclass;

type ref = reference to myclass;

var i: integer;

begin { constructor }
end.

var ar: array 100 of myclass.ref;
    fr: file of myclass.ref;
	pr: ^myclass.ref;
	i:  integer;

function y(r: myclass.ref): myclass.ref; 

begin

   y := r

end;

begin

   for i := 1 to 100 do new(ar[i]);
   rewrite(fr);
   for i := 1 to 100 do write(fr, y(ar[i]));
   new(pr); { get pointer to reference }
   new(pr^); { instantiate new object at reference }
   pr^.i := 10;

end.

The weaving of reference types into other types can be confusing, but it can
help to think of a reference as a simple pointer.

For the last statement above:

   pr^.i := 10;

Think of the structure as a "pointer to a reference to an object of type 
myclass". The pointer indirection "^" refers to the pointer, not the reference.
The statements:

   new(pr); { get pointer to reference }
   new(pr^); { instantiate new object at reference }

Do completely different things. The first allocates a standard pointer, and the
second creates a reference, that happens to be pointed to.

INHERITANCE

The power of classes is that a class can inherit all of the members of another
class:

program test;

class list;

type ref = reference to list;

var next: ref;

procedure gonext; { iterate the list }

begin

   self := self^.next

end;

procedure insert(l: ref); { insert to list }

begin

   next := l; { link this to list }
   l := self { and place on list }

end;

begin { constructor }
end.

class keylist(list);

type ref = reference to keylist;

var key: integer;

function find(l: ref; i: integer): ref;

var r, f: keylist.ref;

begin

   r := l; { index head of list }
   f := nil; { set no object found }
   while r <> nil do begin

      { search objects }
      if r.key = i then begin f := r; r := nil end
      else r.gonext { go next object }

   end;

   find := f { return result }

end;
   
begin { constructor }
end.

var root: keylist.ref; { root of list }
    r:    keylist.ref; { data item }

begin

   root := nil; { clear list }
   new(r); { create new item }
   r.data := 1;
   r.insert(root); { place in list }
   new(r);
   r.data := 4;
   r.insert(root);
   new(r);
   r.data := 10;
   r.insert(root);
   r := find(root, 12)

end.   

The power of objects is that only the class members required to acheive a given
function need be present. A new class that extends the general power of a class
such as the list class to a specific use can inherit the general class. In the
above example, the list class implements list handling in general, and is 
extended by the keylist class that applies the list concept to a specific type
of list, which has integer keys.

This extention can go on to any level. The keylist can be extended to have data
corresponding to its keys, and so on.

REFERENCES AS POINTERS

A reference can be visualised as a pointer to the object. Because a reference
can appear as any variable, it is possible to copy the reference, pass it to
a procedure or function, or imbed it in the object itself. Any number of 
references to a single object can be present.

To understand references, you must think both of the class of object it 
references, as well as the specific object it references.

COST OF OBJECTS

Use of an object vs. use of of record incurs costs, both in the size and the
access time. However, these are minimal. Nevertheless, you must judge if the
extra cost offsets the greater usefulness of objects verses records. Logically,
and object appears to be as a combination of a record and a pointer to that:

program test;

class myclass;

var i: integer;
    b: char;

begin { constructor }
end.

var r: reference to myclass;

begin
end.

Appears as:

   r -> ---------------
        |     i       |
		---------------
		|     b       |
		---------------

The difference between this and an equvalent record is that the reference r must
contain not only a pointer to the object, but also must carry data indicating
what class of the object is. Typically, this is a pointer to a table of class
inheritance relations.

The overhead for use of objects is therefore, the extra space needed to keep the
extra data in the reference, the extra code generated to handle such references,
and the code to check if a given member reference is valid.

There are ways to reduce the cost of objects, as will be discussed.





   











